[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: DNS service policy on SRV1 (BIND/named). The daemon is running and authoritative for jeremysitlab.com, but recursion is disabled.

Mechanism: Clients point to 10.5.0.4 for DNS (e.g., /etc/resolv.conf on pc1/pc2/pc3/laptop1 shows nameserver 10.5.0.4). SRV1’s named.conf explicitly sets “recursion no;” (confirmed via /etc/bind/named.conf). As a result, the server only answers for its own zone and refuses any other lookups, including unqualified short names (e.g., “srv1”) and off-domain names (e.g., www.google.com). From clients, nslookup shows “Got recursion not available” and “server can’t find …: REFUSED” for such queries, matching the “not found” reports. FQDNs within jeremysitlab.com do resolve (e.g., srv1.jeremysitlab.com → 10.5.0.4), confirmed from multiple clients, because the zone is loaded (named-checkconf -z) and named is listening on 10.5.0.4:53.

Localization: SRV1 (10.5.0.4) named process (pid 30), interface eth0, config /etc/bind/named.conf.

Root cause: A recent configuration state on SRV1 disabled DNS recursion, causing clients relying on short names or external resolution to see “not found/REFUSED.”

Corrective fix: Re-enable controlled recursion on SRV1 for campus subnets (e.g., in named.conf: recursion yes; allow-recursion { 10.0.0.0/8; }; optionally add forwarders if upstream exists), or alternatively keep recursion off and push a DNS search suffix “jeremysitlab.com” via DHCP so short names (srv1, printer) expand to FQDNs that SRV1 can authoritatively answer. After changing named.conf, reload named. This will restore expected name resolution behavior from the office. === END ===